 ],
 "literal": "The lord's driven curved-handhold tall chariot / on the clear sea's adjacent shore, clear bells sounding, / to see having come, feeling shy, returns. / Pitiable indeed is love! / Perishing certainly, do I suffer?",
 "translation": "The tall chariot with its curved handhold, driven by the lord of the maritime tract, came so that we might see it, while its clear bells rang on the shore of the clear sea; but then, filled with shyness, it turned back. How pitiable indeed is love! It is destined to perish, and I suffer.",
 "modern": "நெய்தல் நிலத் தலைவன் ஓட்டி வந்த, கொடுஞ்சி கொண்ட நெடிய தேர், தெளிந்த கடலின் கரையில் தெளிவான மணிகள் ஒலிக்க, எங்களைக் காண்பதற்காக வந்து, பின் நாணமடைந்து திரும்பிச் செல்கிறது. காமம் என்பது மிகவும் இரங்கத்தக்கது! அது நிச்சயம் அழியக்கூடியது; அதற்காக நான் வருந்துகிறேன்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine's friend (தோழி) in the Neytal (seashore) landscape. She describes how the hero's tall chariot, decorated with a curved handhold and ringing with clear bells, drove all the way to the shore of the clear sea to visit them, but then turned back out of shyness or hesitation. The friend laments the painful nature of love (காமம்), calling it pitiable and destined to fade away, while expressing her own deep sorrow. The imagery of the chariot arriving and leaving without a meeting highlights the frustration of secret trysts. Key words include கொண்கன் (lord of the coast), நெடுந்தேர் (tall chariot), and தெளிமணி (clear bell).",
